                <pb facs="0248" n="1002" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v02/pg1002"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/5637</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Norwegian Minister</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bryn</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-12-10">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 10, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring further to your note of November
                        13,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d131-f1"/> inquiring whether, under the “Trading
                    with the Enemy Act,” Norwegian business concerns whose commercial intercourse
                    with Germany is limited to the importation of goods from that country, would by
                    reason of that fact be excluded from commerce with the United States, I have the
                    honor to inform you that the War Trade Board to whom your note under
                    acknowledgment was referred for consideration, states that even though the
                    business of the Norwegian concerns be of the nature indicated, they would be
                    regarded as subject to the liabilities provided for in the act in question. Any
                    American firm dealing with a Norwegian concern engaged in business of this
                    character could do so only under license. The granting or withholding of such a
                    license by the War Trade Board would be dependent entirely upon the
                    circumstances of the particular case.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank L. Polk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
